{
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["node"],
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    // The extensions are plain CommonJS on purpose — see README.md and
    // eslint.config.mjs. `node16` module resolution (inherited from the base
    // config) reads that off `package.json`'s absent `"type"` field, so
    // `require`/`module.exports` typecheck as CommonJS without an override
    // here.
    //
    // These two are OFF, and only these two. Measured against the package as
    // it stands: full `strict` reports 107 errors, every one of them an
    // implicit any or an implicit `this` (TS7006 x78, TS2683 x19, and
    // TS7005/7034/7031 for the rest) — not one real type error. Turning them
    // off is therefore a statement about annotation coverage, not about type
    // safety: everything else in `strict` stays on and does catch real
    // mistakes (bad `require`s, wrong arity, wrong property on a value that
    // does have a type).
    //
    // The 19 implicit `this` cases are inherent to the Asciidoctor extension
    // DSL, which rebinds `this` twice — once to the processor being
    // configured, once again inside `process` to the runtime context — so
    // annotating them means a `@this` tag per callback, not a one-line fix.
    //
    // Retiring these: every NEW extension is written fully annotated against
    // `@asciidoctor/core`'s own `types/extensions.d.ts` (see lib/html.js,
    // lib/unique-id.js and lib/warn.js for the shape). Existing files get
    // annotated as they are next touched. When the count reaches zero, delete
    // these two lines — nothing else has to change.
    "noImplicitAny": false,
    "noImplicitThis": false
  },
  "include": ["index.js", "lib/**/*.js"]
}
